  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today Through Monday...\n\nUpper ridging with repeated shortwaves out of the southwest will \nbe the main pattern this week. Hot to very hot temperatures \ncontinue into Wednesday, with an Extreme Heat Warning in effect \nuntil midnight tonight from Stillwater and Golden Valley Counties \nthrough Rosebud and Sheridan Counties. The Extreme Heat Warning in\neffect for Custer, Powder River, Fallon, and Carter Counties \nremains in effect until Wednesday night. Look for high \ntemperatures across the area in the upper 80s to 100F (warmest in \nthe east). Low temperatures in southeast Montana will be warm \ntonight, in the upper 60s to low 70s, further adding to cumulative\nheat stress.\n\nThe focus continues to transition towards a warm and moist setup \ninto the weekend, as deep moisture continues to stream into the \nregion from the southwest. Exceptionally high precipitable water \nvalues are shown by models and ensembles. This will result in the \nthreat for heavy to very heavy rainfall with showers and \nthunderstorms the next few days. The first wave of precipitation \narrives this afternoon and overnight with a shortwave. The latest\nHi-Res models are showing heavy showers and a few thunderstorms \ndeveloping over the mountains and foothills west of Billings late \nthis afternoon/evening and moving eastward into the central and \nnorth zones around midnight. There is a Marginal Risk (1/5) for \nstrong to severe thunderstorms over the west-half of the forecast \narea today. There is also a Slight Risk (2/5) over the northwest \nzones. The primary risks are heavy rainfall and gusty winds up to \n65 mph. Hail up to quarter size is a secondary risk.\n\nIn addition to the thunderstorm risk, the Weather Prediction \nCenter has a Marginal Risk (at least 5% risk) for excessive \nrainfall from Billings to Livingston and north. A Flash Flood \nWatch remains in effect until 3 AM MDT Wednesday, for areas along \nand north of I-90 from Livingston to Billings. Rainfall rates in \nexcess of 1 inch per hour are possible (moderate to high chance) \nwith the heaviest showers and storms. The most likely timing for \nstorms today/tonight is 6 PM MDT to 3 AM MDT Wednesday. \n\nWednesday will be similar with slow-moving afternoon showers and \nstorms lasting into the overnight hours. The threat for strong to \nsevere thunderstorms is low though, with weaker instability and \nminimal shear. The threat for excessive rainfall exists once \nagain, with another Marginal Risk including and west of a \nSheridan County to Musselshell County line. An additional Flash \nFlood Watch has been issued for these areas from Noon MDT \nWednesday to 3 AM MDT Thursday. The most likely timing based on \nthe most recent Hi-Res runs is 2 PM MDT Wednesday to 12 AM MDT\nThursday.\n\nA wind shift and breezy conditions will accompany the storms \ntonight and through early Wednesday morning. Look for gusts out of\nthe north-northwest in the 20s and 30s mph across the west-\ncentral areas.\n\nModels continue to show a stationary front developing near the \nDakotas border tonight through Wednesday evening. This looks to \nkeep southeasterly and dry flow into southern Montana. Relative \nhumidity values will be in the teens to low 20s percent. Winds do \nnot look to be that strong so the need for fire weather highlights\nis low, though the risk for grass fires will be elevated.\n\nAnomalous moisture and unsettled flow continues through the \nweekend, with daily chances for afternoon/evening showers and \nthunderstorms across the western and central areas. Look for high \ntemperatures in the 80s and 90s through the weekend.\n\nMatos",
